Z||000016||Monkeyman||09-26-2001||08:38 PM||||I don't know anything about gas in the oil (or was it oil in the gas?) but I just want to comment on the poor attitude regarding dealerships.  My 1<sup>st</sup> Fiero always went to a private garage (the only one in town who was willing to work on Fieros).  After getting ripped off more times than I care to count and finally realizing that the mechanics knew absolutely nothing about Fieros (can you say "coolant tube X <B>3</B>"?), I finally decided to hit the dealership.  I have <I>never</I> had the slightest complaint about them.  They work with me on anything.  They always do competent work and if they're unsure about something (CC wiring isn't their specialty), they let me know instead of charging me the going rate for 20 hours of "diagnostics".  BTW--In case anyone has any mechanical problems around my neck of the woods, try Dorais Chevrolet in Wabash, IN.  Tell 'em I sent ya.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||85 gt fiero||09-26-2001||08:56 PM||||i had that same experience it seems that dealers do better work, but if you were to go there just for a part a may be alittle on the high side, hope you get it fixed.||||reg||1
Z||000018||dennis_6||09-26-2001||10:12 PM||||I usualy work on my cars myself, but when you replace everything you can think of you have to turn to someone.  I am with Monkeyman private garages wonder why people stuffed the Fiero's engine in the trunk.  So I stay away from them at all cost.  Bush has always done my family right so I took it there.  They said they thought it needed a new engine.  As many problems as this engine has had their probaly right.  I just hope I can get a cheap replacement engine till I am ready and have money for a V8 swap.||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierospeeder||09-27-2001||03:22 AM||||I would try on working on the car myself.<P>If the car runs for a few minutes perfectly, then dies later.  You probably have an EFI problem.<P>You can go to murry's car parts and rent a scantool and see the real time info on your car||||reg||1
Z||000020||Fformula88||09-27-2001||01:12 PM||||I have no doubt there are honest dealers out there doing great work on Fiero's like the one monkeyman recommended... <P>Here's my experience...<P>Took my car to Jim Murphy Pontiac in Depew NY to have an engine code 32 (egr) looked at more closely and an oil leak diagnosed.  Mind you my whole family had dealt with them numerous times in the past... all satisfied with their repair work.<P>Got a call that it was the pan gasket and that the engine light diagnosis revealed the car was running bad because of a cracked ignition coil and old plug wires.<P>I was like WTF  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> I just did a tune up like a month ago and the coil and wires were brand new!  Also it ran ok when I took it in except for it stalling when it idled and was cold, but ran fine when driven except for the mysterious engine light which I also knew was the EGR system.  I told them to go ahead and replace the pan gasket, and not tuch the ignition.  since the stuff was new, and if it was bad I would take it up with the parts store.  I doubted it was bad.<P>When I picked it up they told me they had put on a couple used wires to replace the really bad ones.  I started it up and took it out and something new was definately wrong!  It was missing all over and barely drove.  Opened the trunk and I could see it arcing from where the dist. to coil wire goes on the coil and from two of their used wires they put on.<P>I went right back and requested my wires.  Looked like they took a file and totalley shredded the plug boot on one of them!  After much arguing I got no where so I took it all home to inspect it further (luckily I lived around the corner).<P>Closer inspection of my so called bad coil revealed it was fine.  I first installed the old coil and amazingly, it arc'd at the same exact spot!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">  They poked a hole in the dist-coil wire to make it arc at the coil and make it appear bad!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  They also installed bad wires to make the problem appear worse!  Also the car still leaked just as much oil!<P>In the end, I got them in a little hot water with the DMV about the oil leak which they agreed to work on again.  I couldn't get anywhere with the wires since it was "my word against theirs".  <P>Point being, they purposely damaged parts on my car to fatten the invoice!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  <P>I also know of a Caddy/Olds dealer locally that takes the new parts for customers cars and installs them in their personal cars, putting the used parts in the customers cars!  This is from someone who used to work there!<P>As a result I wouldn't trust any dealership!  Its also why I do as much of my own work as possible now!!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero2m6siga.JPG">       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000021||dennis_6||09-27-2001||03:31 PM||||First off the SKS is $250 dollars and details can be seen at: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/dennis_6/sks.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/dennis_6/sks.html</A>  <P>Call your local gunstore and try to get a deal like this, a stock a SKS with garbage would stock and non detachable 5 round clip will cost you $220 dollars before tax! Close to $300 after and this is price for a used rifle not a new one!!!!<P>Here is exactly what my car is doing:<BR>Won't start unless you hold all the way down on the gas pedal and then as soon as you let go the vehicle dies, the car with the pedal to the floor will not excide 1k in park and runs like it only has one cylinder working.<P>What I know:<BR>Spark is going to every cylinder<BR>Throttle cable is ok<BR>IAC is ok<BR>TPS is ok<BR>Vacuum tubes are ok<BR>Cold start valve is ok<BR>Distrbutor and Spark plug wires are ok<BR>Plenum is ok<BR>EGR is ok<P>I really think it is time to pull the engine!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||dennis_6||09-27-2001||03:37 PM||||Formula 88 - I know there are alot of crooked dealers Jerry Haag screwed me everytime I took my old Chevy Corsica to the shop.  One time they broke a brand new tensioner and said it was old and worn out and then made me pay 100 dollars just to replace a brand new part they broke.  I was mad.  So I started working on my own cars.  Bush seems pretty good though.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Raydar||09-27-2001||04:19 PM||||Dennis,<P>Starting the engine with the gas pushed all the way down puts the ECM in "clear flood" mode. It pulses the injectors for just a tiny amount of gas. That, and the throttle being wide open, is what clears the excess fuel and lets the engine start.<BR>Something is flooding your engine. (This is what caused the gas in the oil.) <BR>If you are sure the cold start valve is good, check the sensor that it talks to. They can go bad, and tell the cold start valve that it's like -40 degrees. Will dump in *mucho* fuel. Just for grins, try disconnecting the cold start valve, completely. I'm pretty sure your engine is not shot. As soon as you fix the flooding problem, change the oil. The gas in the oil is dangerous, and is not doing your bearings or rings any good.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000024||dennis_6||09-27-2001||04:45 PM||||How do I disconnect the cold start valve?<BR>By coldstart valve do you mean injector. Do you mean just pull the electrical connector? If I pull out the injector gas is gonna go everywhere.  Please explain.||||reg||1
Z||000025||dennis_6||09-27-2001||04:46 PM||||How do I disconnect the cold start valve?<BR>By coldstart valve do you mean injector. Do you mean just pull the electrical connector? If I pull out the injector gas is gonna go everywhere.  Please explain.||||reg||1
Z||000026||dennis_6||09-27-2001||05:12 PM||||Pulled the coldstart injector wires left fuel line attached and the car turned over a litte easier but still did the same thing, one other thing when you hold your foot down to keep the car running about 5 seconds later you get a muffled backfire sound and the engine dies.  Please help!||||reg||1
Z||000027||Raydar||09-27-2001||09:20 PM||||With the cold start injector disabled, do you still have to hold the gas on the floor to start it? <P>Just for grins, now try holding the gas down about 1/4 throttle. If it's what I'm guessing, it now won't be getting <I>enough</I> gas. The part-throttle opening will pulse the injectors "on" longer for a cold start.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
